@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant — the profile DEFINITION (stereotypes extending metaclasses) contrasted with its APPLICATION (a regular class decorated with the stereotype).
'   usecase:      Contrast meta-level (profile definition) with model level (profile application to user elements).
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       Software Architecture
' ============================================================================
title Example Profile Diagram Variant - Profile Application

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TOP frame: PROFILE DEFINITION (meta-level).
'   Stereotype «Service» extends metaclass Component and declares the tagged
'   value endpoint : String.  This is part of the profile; nothing here is a
'   real component yet.
'
' BOTTOM frame: PROFILE APPLICATION (model level).
#   The ChargeGateway class is an ordinary user-level Component.  Applying
'   the stereotype «Service» decorates it and lets the model carry a concrete
'   tagged value (endpoint = "/v1/charge").  The stereotype name appears in
'   guillemets on the element; the tagged value appears as «stereotype» slot.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ===== DEFINITION (meta-level) ==========================================
package "MicroserviceProfile" <<profile>> as defProfile {
  class Component << (M,#B4A7E5) metaclass >> as defComp { }

  class Service << (S,#F2C9C9) stereotype >> as defSvc {
    + endpoint : String
  }

  defSvc ..> defComp : <<extend>>
}

' ===== APPLICATION (model level) ========================================
package "BillingModel" <<model>> as appModel {
  class "«Service»\nChargeGateway" as gw << (C,#FFF2CC) component >> {
    endpoint = "/v1/charge"
    + charge(amount : Money) : Result
  }
}

note top of defSvc
  <b>PROFILE DEFINITION (meta-level)</b>
  Defines WHAT stereotypes exist and
  which tagged values they carry.
  No real element here — this is the
  schema of the extension.
end note

note top of gw
  <b>PROFILE APPLICATION (model level)</b>
  ChargeGateway is an ordinary user
  Component.  Applying «Service»
  decorates it with the stereotype
  label and lets the model record a
  concrete tagged value
  endpoint = "/v1/charge".
end note

note bottom of appModel
  <b>Meta-level vs model level</b>
  The profile DEFINITION lives one meta-level above the model.  It says
  "a Service is a Component with an endpoint tag."  The APPLICATION uses
  that definition on a real element: "ChargeGateway IS a Service and its
  endpoint is /v1/charge."  A profile with no application shapes nothing.
end note

@enduml
